{
  "title": "Calcaneovalgus Foot",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4067/calcaneovalgus-foot",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 21 2022",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Calcaneovalgus Foot is a common acquired condition caused by intrauterine \"packaging\" seen in neonates that presents as a benign soft tissue contracture deformity of the foot characterized by hindfoot eversion and dorsiflexion.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with a calcaneovalgus foot deformity without congenital deformity or dislocation.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually observation with passive stretching and possible casting as the condition resolves spontaneously in 3-6 months.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mild form can occur in up to 40% of newborns",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "estimated incidence of true deformity is 1 in 1,000 live births",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more common in females and first-born children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "usually a positional deformity of the foot caused by **intrauterine \"packaging\"**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "excessively dorsiflexed hindfoot that is **passively correctable**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "dorsal surface of foot can rest on anterior tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4067/images/calcaeovalgus_foot.jpg",
                  "caption": "Clinical photo showing a calcaneovalgus foot"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "looks similar to vertical talus",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "differs on exam in that vertical talus has a rigid hindfoot equinus/valgus and rigid dorsiflexion through midfoot",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "vertical talus also has mid-foot valgus, with a medial prominant talar head.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "AP and lateral tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "used to determine presence of posteromedial bowing",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**plantar flexion lateral radiographs**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "useful to differentiate from congenital vertical talus",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "if foot is sufficiently flexible to rule out congenital vertical talus, no radiographs are required",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "before ossification of navicular at age 3, the first metatarsal is used as a proxy for the navicular on radiographic evaluation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "calcaneovalgus foot",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "first metatarsal **will line up with talus**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "vertical talus",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "the axis of the talus passes plantar to the 1st metatarsal (and navicular if visible yet) on both standard lateral and plantar flexion lateral radiographs",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4067/images/meary%20and%20talocal%20labeled_moved.jpg",
                              "caption": ""
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Differential",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Conditions that should be differentiated",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**posteromedial tibial bowing**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "posteromedial bowing of the tibia is almost alway accompanied by some degree of calcaneovalgus foot deformity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "with the calcaneovalgus foot, apex of the deformity is at the ankle joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4067/images/cv_foot.jpg",
                      "caption": "Clinical pic"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "with posteromedial bowing of the tibia, apex is at the distal tibia",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4067/images/calcaneovalgus-foot.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**congenital vertical talus (CVT)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "appears similar to calcaneovalgus foot clinically",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "with CVT, the hindfoot is in equinus",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "hindfoot is in calcaneus (dorsiflexion) in a calcaneovalgus foot",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "with CVT, there is a midfoot dislocation through the talonavicular joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "CVT is a rigid deformity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "calcaneovalgus is a flexible deformity",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**paralytic foot deformity**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "deformity is caused by",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "spasticity of",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "**foot dorsiflexors (L4 and L5)/evertors (S1)**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "weakness of",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "**plantar flexors (S1 and S2) /inverters (L5)**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "this muscle imbalance can be caused by an **L5 spinal bifida leading to a calcaneovalgus deformity**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "observation & passive stretching exercises",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "if foot can be plantar-flexed beyond neutral",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "resolution may be expedited by stretching performed by parents",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "typically resolves spontaneously by 3-6 months",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "casting",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "if foot cannot be plantar-flexed beyond neutral",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "severity of initial deformity has no relation to final outcome",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Leg Length Discrepancy**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "LLD a possible sequela when calcaneovalgus foot is associated with posteromedial bowing of the tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "the most common surgery needed for posterior medial bowing is to address the LLD",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Flexible flatfoot deformity**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs several years after the resolution of the foot deformity",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
